Date: Sat, 20 Nov 1993 19:03:16 -0800
From: tribble@netcom.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: How Voluntary Socialism is Disproven Rationally

	 Nancy Lebovitz writes"

	 > I'm not sure that Tim has disproven all possible forms of socialism
	 > by bringing up the calculation problem, since after the Singularity,
	 > people will be able to calculate much better. Is there a proof
	 > that the complexity of what people want and will do about it is
	 > *always* going to be greater than their calculational ability?

The agoric work is marvelous arrow to lay the computation debate to
rest.  Agoric computation means using market institutions internal to
the computer to manage computational resources.  Even for relatively
simple problems (sceduling processor time for a handful of processes)
they can outperform traditional schemes (ref, the Spawn project at
Xerox PARC, and a few others).

A proof of sorts is relatively trivial.  There are a few simple
allocation problems in which finding an "optimal" solution is
exponential (or at least NP).  An example is allocating memory among
caches (each cache independently trades memory for processor-time).
The marginal value of adding memory to any given cache depends on the
states of the other caches (if A calls B, thens A's cache affects how
often B's cache gets used, and thus affects the marginal payoff for
investing memory in caching for B).  This problem is sufficiently hard
that current computer systems almost don't even try to trade caches
off.  The size of such problems grow with the size of the computer
systems, so they get as much bigger ashardware gets faster.  Since
solving them is exponential, as computers get faster and bigger, the
diffculty of solving the resource management problem gets *more*
difficult (and more valuable) relative to the speed of the hardware,
not less difficult.

A more fundamental problem with central planning is that it assumes
there's some optimum, which assumes a goal to optimize for.  These
assumptions simply make no sense in the context of independent agents
like humans.  Even if one could postulate such a goal, and convince
lots of people it was a good idea, most people will still have other
purposes, and thus break the assumption of optimizing a single goal.

