|> recommend a book I would appreciate it. I just read an excellent
|> article by Bruce Schneier about the "IDEA" encryption algorithm
|> and he just wrote a book "Applied Cryptography". Is it any good?

Schneier's _Applied Cryptography_ has the virtues of accessibility,
practicality, and currency. A few topics seem to be treated lightly,
but I believe it fills a vacancy in the literature. I haven't had the
book very long and I certainly haven't perused all of the sections,
but I recommend it highly as either a handbook (especially if you get
the source code disks) or an entree to the field, for people with a
programming background but maybe not tons of abstract math. If you
decide to get deeper into the literature, the book has hundreds of
references to the deeper literature :-).

Downsides are: it's a paperback at hardcover prices; some very
specific picky subtopics are hard to find detail on; and when you get
right down to it, most books-with-disks cost about this much (FRN$45)
and you don't have to cough up another FRN$30 for the disks, which
consist largely of code he scarfed from the net.

