From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Investment, Philanthropy, and Oceania

I'd like to wish the best of luck to Tim May in his investment 
and Derek Zahn in his project, and the many other Extropians
in their various efforts.

Like many here I'm most interested in the software business, 
and it's important to realize that mass market software not
the dominant marketing avenue.  Most programmers work in some kind 
of "bundled" industry (my own background includes systems
programming, putting out operating systems for the RS/6000,
Sequent, etc.)  My main exposure to mass market software
was putting out programmer's tools for the PC (Intersolv).
Mass market software quickly turns into publishing --
writing and bundling manuals, customer support, sales, copyright
enforcement, etc.  The fraction of the staff doing programming
at a software company like Microsoft isn't that much different
than at a hardware company like Intel (and the total fraction
of engineers is much higher at the latter).  It gets pretty
weird; at Intersolv the technical writers were _de facto_ designing 
the software!   

Many companies big and small make the mistake of 
designing things the way they "should be" and trying to
solve the big picture, instead of trying to solve people's
practical problems.  Even the big software breakthroughs like 
the spreadsheet started out as little more than trying to
solve some straightforward practical problems.

The whole area of "cypherspace", Habitat and graphical
MUDs, Internet commerce, etc. interests me greatly.
Again there's much talk of "realistic VR", "the architecture
of cyberspace",  "standards for Internet commerce", and the
the like, but what practical problems are being solved?  What actual 
end user products (as opposed to tools for other developers)
are being written and sold?  There seems to be much talk and little 
action about all that, so I'd like to change tune and explore what 
folks are actually doing, rather than designing/dreaming about.

Over the last year, coinciding with my entry to Extropians I've
done quite a bit of the conceptual thinking, design, dabbling
and prototyping on a wide variety of software projects (and
other stuff, like the comet mining scenarios, all these posts
to sci.space and Extropians, and on and on!  This is your
brain on Future Rapture!)

Alas for my pocketbook this is all quite the opposite of doing 
what I'm traditionally best at, namely getting products out the door.  
Nevertheless, it's been great to have been able to "go meta", get 
away from the bureaucratic grind (real-world programming is 99% 
bureacracy, filling out the grammars other people have made, but if 
you're good at it filling them out in useful ways you can make tons 
more $$$ than the folks who design forms).

Some of my recent projects are along the lines of bridging the gap 
between the abstract crypto/privacy/security algorithms and concepts 
running around, and the practical freindly integration
of such a "cypherspace" on today's desktop and various 
large, straighforward markets.  Starting out with
a humble, straightforward PGP GUI (stupid -ea flag keeps
hanging the system under Windows, grrr...) and the like.
With this perhaps, I'm edging closer to "practical product space"
and weeding out the rest the prototyping projects, so if you see me
"blip off the radar screen", don't get too concerned -- it probably
means I'm actually back to doing some real work!

So, I've heard quite a bit about the grandiose
dreams, (and of course spieled on about mine!), I've heard
about "what kinds of companies succeed" (succinct answer
-- companies that work on things people pay for, instead
of things that make them fit the profile of "companies
that tend to succeed"!).  Now I'd like to hear about some of 
the humble, straightforward things folks are _writing_ or
_building_ right now (not just designing).  What stuff is really 
in the works to be spit out some time in 1994.  Freely mixing in 
various mileaus I'm involved in, here's some software stuff
people I know are working on:

* "Secure Disk" and other projects toward the raid-proof PC
(no more Steve Jacksons!)
* Various goings-on in text MUDs
* Habitat and possible successors
* Joule (conquering the world with a new language _and_ OS!)
* Genetic programming for finance, horse race gambling, 
protein sequence classification, robot planning, Go, etc.
* "Hand" financial planning to the max, with lots
of RAM and compiled spreadsheets.
* Computational chemistry/nanotech simulation of various
sorts (MOIL, Crystal Clear/diamondoid, etc.)
* integretation of PGP into mailers, PGP GUIs, etc.
* remailer tweaks (mostly talk these days, alas).
* conquering cypherspace with TCL
* A bunch of stuff I'm under non-disclosure for. :-)

That's just what comes to mind this evening, I've probably
missed some important ones.

