Agreed, the prototypical "one man shop" is a valid start-up model. But
usually not for other investors, as the start-up is just too small to
support the paperwork needed to issue shares, etc. (_Partnerships_ are
the norm at this size, where, for example, Albert has the idea and
skill and Biff puts up some money and they each own part.)

> I believe that the continuing expansion of computing, networking,
> and the information economy creates many such opportunities for
> individuals and small groups looking for entrepreneurial niches.
> Many of us will participate in more than one such venture, creating
