> at about the right level of difficulty for the resources you have
> available, it's probably actually too difficult!  I am beginning
> to wonder whether my major effort at present isn't biting off more
> than I can chew).

My hunch is that about 95% of such attempts to find an entrepreneurial
niche are destined to fail: for every "DiskDoubler" there are 20 or 30
frustrated software writers unable to find the right idea, unable to
get the programming finished, unable to ultimately find distribution
channels.

This doesn't mean it's hopeless, just that great care must be taken to
avoid being one of the 95% that fails. Studying the successes--and the
failures. Of course, failures are generally less publicized, except by
direct word of mouth by their friends, which is one reason I may
appear to be "picking on" my friends at AMIX and Xanadu...far from
picking on them, I just think we have a good opportunity to learn
something from how they did things.  Especially since at least three
readers of Extropians--Tribble, Morningstar, Dinkelacker--were closely
involved. Jamie has already commented.

> This isn't anything new, really.  For example, a freelance writer
> fits the model of the tiny start-up quite well:  buy a typewriter
> and some paper, and get to work.  1993 version:  buy a computer
> and a modem, and get to work.  Of course, just as a freelance

The freelance writing model represents two distinct situations:

1. A contract writer, doing technical documentation or other "spec"
writing. This is completely analogous to the ordinary software
consultant. Indeed, it's often hard to tell them apart.

Good pay, flexible hours.

2. The would-be novelist or successful book writer. Here, this is
analogous to the entrepreneur hoping for a home run, a "DiskDoubler"
type of hit. And for every Tom Clancy or John Grisham, there are
hundreds of frustrated novelists.

Lousy pay, even more flexible hours. But don't quit your day job. And
don't expect anyone else to fund you.

> That is, if Romana Machado decided to sell her new program "Stego",
> would there be any buyers?  Does anybody know if there is a functioning
> infrastructure for matching up people with needs to the "mini-apps"
> that are becoming more and more common?  I know that "shareware"
> exists, and contrary to most people I think it works quite well
> for all concerned, but it's still too hard for people to find the
> programs they want.

Selling of such "mini-apps" was in fact a primary raison d'etre for
AMIX, which is even more of a reason for us to look carefully at why
AMIX evolved as it did. And whether Net-based services of a similar
nature could work. (Well, we know the Net will someday be used to buy
and sell goods and services--and is to some extent already. What we
don't know yet is what form this will take, what form of cash or
payment system will be used, what the laws will be. This is a huge
topic unto itself.)

> I like this model for a freelance computerist rather better than
> the "contractor" model because it leaves room for the tiny
> entrepreneur to implement his or her own vision and sell it
> rather than implementing the vision of a customer with a
> specific need (and often having only one or two people ever
> even use the resulting program).  Does it sound possible?

I agree this is the most attractive approach for an individual. The
small project allows nimbleness, evolutionary changes in response to
customer feedback, and low cash burn rate.

The problem is that unless the product starts selling, no cash
whatsover comes in. Thus, the 95% end up burning their savings (or
their evening hours) with nothing to show for it.

This is an advantage as well, in terms of motivation, but should not
be ignored. The fact is that most such small software efforts will
never, ever get distribution channels and will sink without a trace
beneath the cruel waves.

There may be some synergistic effect in having several of these
software authors all contributing ideas and code, with the idea of
commercializing the best ideas. This was exactly how Autodesk itself
got started...a group of programmers kicked around ideas and launched
several projects, with the explicit intention of pursuing whichever
ones appeared to generate market interest. Several of the ideas went
nowhere, but "AutoCad" succeeded in a spectacular way.

The Extropians list has some very bright people, with experience in a
wide range of areas, and so this model might be very interesting.

Also, within a few years we should have fairly good video conferencing
capabilities (I hear the Mac Quadra 840av already has adequate
capabilities...maybe some Pentium machines do as well) and so
"distributed cooperatives" can make sense. Derek in Wisconsin, others
in D.C., others in Huntsville, etc., could "meet" in a forum that
allows virtual communities.

(Pavel Curtis of Xerox, working on MOOs and MUDs, says that studies
show the telephone is by far the most important step. So video
teleconferencing may be nice, but not actually needed. I tend to think
seeing one's partners is very important.)

Well, I'm now drifting into other topics, so I'll end now.

Just let me close by saying that the Extropian on-line community could
turn out to produce some interesing things.

--Tim May


