Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Investment, Philanthropy, and Oceania

Nick Szabo (X-Message-Number: #93-11-790) makes several excellent points
regarding the software business, especially how it can quickly become a
publishing operation.

He makes the following point, that I'd like to take the liberty of extending:

>I've heard
>about "what kinds of companies succeed" (succinct answer
>-- companies that work on things people pay for, instead
>of things that make them fit the profile of "companies
>that tend to succeed"!). 

I couldn't agree with him more, but there's more to it than this. I'm
mainly interested in startups that go for a big play. In my work here in
Silicon Valley, I've come across several companies that are making things
that people do pay for, but the companies' execution is so poor that they
are dying a slow death from a starvation revenue stream. Aside from the
founders, this makes it hard to keep other talent onboard. Sometimes they
leave to create other competitive startups.

Creating awareness and efficient distribution can be a bitch to accomplish,
and are often limiting factors that cap revenue. I've noticed that
companies that say "we believe in word of mouth instead of advertising" or
"we don't want to denigrate ourselves by using PR people" often are the
same ones who are very cash-tight. And these are companies that have things
people would gladly pay for if the target market knew about them. Some do,
yes, and many founders are satisfied with making good incomes for a few
people. And there's nothing wrong with this approach at all. 

Also, I'm not addressing OEM or "Adobe" strategies here. [Watch this space.]

Granted, much of this has to do with the size of one's aspirations. I'm not
a fan of subsistence living. 

